# Old HubSpot URLs. Order matters — Netlify stops at the first match.
# The blog now lives at /writing, so legacy /blog links land there rather than
# on the homepage — closer to what the visitor was actually looking for.
#
# /contact used to redirect here too, to the homepage's #notify anchor — that
# was fine when there was no real contact page. The Four Laws site merge
# (see docs/site-consolidation.md) added a real one at /contact, so that rule
# was removed rather than silently shadowing the new page.

/blog                /writing    301
/blog/*               /writing   301
/starter-package     /           301
/premium-package     /           301

# Site-consolidation merge (Four Laws + main site into one Astro project,
# build.format switched from 'directory' to 'file' sitewide — see
# docs/site-consolidation.md). This site's own pages used to serve with a
# trailing slash (/writing/, /writing/<slug>/, /about/); anyone with an old
# link, bookmark, or search-indexed URL in that form should still land
# correctly rather than 404. Four Laws' own URLs never had a trailing slash —
# that's exactly what this whole merge was careful to preserve — so they need
# no equivalent rule here.
#
# Individual posts (/writing/<slug>/) deliberately have NO explicit rule
# below: Netlify's splat (*) can only sit at the end of a pattern, so
# "/writing/*/ -> /writing/:splat" isn't expressible as a single working rule
# — it would just echo the trailing slash straight back. Relying instead on
# Netlify's documented pretty-URL normalization (a request for
# /writing/foo/ falls through to the /writing/foo.html asset the same way
# /writing/foo does) — confirm this actually holds on the real deploy before
# trusting it; if it doesn't, this needs Netlify Edge Functions or one
# explicit rule per post rather than a guessed-at generic pattern.
/writing/             /writing    301
/about/               /about      301
/contact/             /contact    301
/thanks/              /thanks     301
